WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.974
COUNT star is not wrong It counts rows exactly as
requested The

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.026
problem is that the joined row may not represent the
event you

00:00:09.053 --> 00:00:14.636
think it represents After joining several
one-to-many tables one

00:00:14.662 --> 00:00:18.202
row can represent a combination of an admission a
lab test and

00:00:18.228 --> 00:00:22.757
a prescription Counting those rows does not directly
count any one of

00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:28.443
those event types COUNT DISTINCT on an event
identifier can protect

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:34.183
a simple count For example counting distinct
admission IDs can recover admission

00:00:34.209 --> 00:00:40.095
count even after other detail tables are joined
However pre-aggregating

00:00:40.108 --> 00:00:43.108
is often easier to reason about when the final grain
is one

00:00:43.135 --> 00:00:48.150
row per patient You can summarize admissions to one
patient row summarize

00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:53.439
labs to one patient row and then join those
summaries The safest

00:00:53.465 --> 00:00:58.662
approach is to name the measure explicitly
admissions lab tests prescriptions patients

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.836
or joined combinations Then choose the counting
method that matches that meaning
